    32	---
    33	
    34	## Statement
    35	
    36	Kelley (1950) derived the Axiom of Choice from Tychonoff's theorem as follows.
    37	Given nonempty sets $X_a$ for $a \in I$, adjoin a point $\infty$ to each and
    38	topologise $X_a \cup \{\infty\}$ by the **cofinite** topology; each such space is
    39	compact, the sets $X_a$ are claimed to be closed, and the finite intersection
    40	property of the family $\{\pi_a^{-1}(X_a)\}$ then yields a point of the product,
    41	that is, a choice function.
    42	
    43	**Schechter (2006): that argument does not prove the Axiom of Choice.** In the
    44	cofinite topology on $X_a \cup \{\infty\}$ the set $X_a$ is **not** closed when
    45	$X_a$ is infinite, since every nonempty open set is cofinite and therefore meets
    46	$X_a$. What Kelley's specialisation actually proves, and is equivalent to over
    47	ZF, is the **Boolean prime ideal theorem**.
    48	
    49	**The repair.** Make the adjoined point **isolated**: topologise
    50	$X_a \cup \{\infty\}$ as the disjoint sum of $X_a$ with its cofinite topology and
    51	the one-point space $\{\infty\}$, so that the open sets are the unions of a
    52	cofinite subset of $X_a$ (or $\emptyset$) with a subset of $\{\infty\}$. This
    53	space is compact and $T_1$, and now $X_a$ genuinely is closed, its complement
    54	$\{\infty\}$ being open. With the repair the classical conclusion survives in the
    55	form: **"every product of compact $T_1$ spaces is compact" is equivalent to the
    56	Axiom of Choice over ZF**, while "every product of compact Hausdorff spaces is
    57	compact" is equivalent to BPI.
    58	
    59	## Remarks
    60	
    61	- **Not proved in this library.** Neither Tychonoff's theorem nor either
    62	  equivalence is proved here.
    63	
    64	- **What would prove it.** A ZF development of product topologies and
    65	  compactness, plus the filter arguments of the ultrafilter lemma. Only the
    66	  *independence* half, that BPI does not imply the Axiom of Choice, needs
    67	  forcing; that half is [[rem-halpern-levy-bpi-not-ac]]. The rest is elementary
    68	  once topology exists, which is why this item records a correction rather than a
    69	  deep theorem.
    70	
    71	- **Why it matters here.** The claim "Tychonoff implies AC" is standard, and the
    72	  standard proof of it is the one Kelley gave. A library that repeats it verbatim
    73	  would be asserting an equivalence with [[def-axiom-of-choice]] on the strength
    74	  of a false lemma, and would also be mis-pricing the Hausdorff case, which costs
    75	  only [[thm-ultrafilter-lemma]]. When the topology track reaches Tychonoff, this
    76	  correction is what keeps the two statements and their two prices apart in
    77	  [[rem-choice-ledger]].
    78	
    79	- **Conditional discipline.** This item states an error and its repair, plus two
    80	  ZF equivalences; only the accompanying strictness claim, that BPI is genuinely
    81	  weaker, is an independence result and it is recorded conditionally in
    82	  [[rem-halpern-levy-bpi-not-ac]].
