    29	
    30	## Three conventions, fixed once
    31	
    32	**1. Compact means the open-cover condition and nothing more.** Following
    33	Bourbaki, some authors reserve *compact* for a space that is both quasicompact,
    34	meaning every open cover has a finite subcover, and Hausdorff, and then say
    35	*quasicompact* for the cover condition alone. This library takes the more widely
    36	adopted convention: [[def-compact-space]] defines *compact* as the cover
    37	condition, the word *quasicompact* is not used, and every Hausdorff hypothesis is
    38	written into the statement that needs it — as in
    39	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]] and
    40	[[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]. A reader arriving from the other
    41	convention should read every unqualified "compact" here as "quasicompact".
    42	
    43	**2. Compactness of a subset is intrinsic.** [[def-compact-space]] calls
    44	$A \subseteq X$ compact when the subspace $A$ carries is a compact space, not when
    45	every family of open subsets of $X$ covering $A$ has finitely many members
    46	covering it. The two conditions agree, and that is a theorem,
    47	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]]; no proof here uses the ambient
    48	reading without citing it. Compactness belongs to $A$ together with its
    49	topology. It is preserved under a homeomorphic realization as a subspace, but a
    50	different ambient may induce a different topology and a different compactness
    51	answer. The metric development fixed
    52	the same reading, and that the two developments describe one notion is
    53	[[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
    54	
    55	**Relative compactness is the exception**, and deliberately so:
    56	[[def-compactness-variants]] defines $A$ to be relatively compact in $X$ when
    57	$\overline{A}$ is compact, and the closure is taken in $X$. That condition really
    58	is about $A$ inside $X$ and changes when the ambient space changes.
    59	
    60	**3. A separation axiom is written out rather than named where it is not
    61	available.** Claim 4 of [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]] needs every
    62	singleton to be closed. That condition is a named separation axiom, and the page
    63	naming it is not among this page's declared prerequisites, so the hypothesis is
    64	stated in the vocabulary of open and closed sets and nothing is asserted about the
    65	axiom it belongs to. On this page a neighbourhood need not be open, and the
    66	intersection of no sets is the whole space; these two general conventions are in
    67	force without further comment.
    68	
    69	## The choice ledger
    70	
    71	Every entry below is a statement about **the proof given in this library**, and
    72	about nothing else. Each is an upper bound on what that proof spends; no item on
    73	this page claims that a choice principle is *necessary*, because that would be an
    74	independence result and this library proves none.
    75	
    76	**Theorems of ZF, spending no choice principle at all.**
    77	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]],
    78	[[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]], [[thm-compact-iff-fip]],
    79	[[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]],
    80	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]],
    81	[[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]],
    82	[[lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor]],
    83	[[thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces]],
    84	[[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]],
    85	[[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]],
    86	[[thm-one-point-compactification-properties]],
    87	[[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]],
    88	[[thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space]], claim 1 of
    89	[[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]], claims 1 and 2 of
    90	[[thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness]], and claims 1 and 3 of
    91	[[thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact]].
    92	
    93	Where a proof in that list does make a selection, the selection is over a finite
    94	index set, and [[lem-finite-choice]] is itself a theorem of ZF. Two of those
    95	proofs avoid even that: [[lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor]] indexes its cover
    96	by *pairs* of open sets, so the compactness criterion hands back the second
    97	entries with the indices, and
    98	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]] collects the family of
    99	**all** open sets that work rather than choosing one for each point. The textbook
   100	phrase "for each $y \in K$ choose disjoint open $U_y, V_y$" is a selection over an
   101	arbitrary index set, that is the full Axiom of Choice
   102	([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), and it is avoided throughout this page.
   103	
   104	**Spending the Axiom of Choice, through Zorn's lemma
   105	([[thm-zorn]]).** [[thm-alexander-subbase-lemma]] spends it exactly once, to
   106	obtain a maximal open cover without a finite subcover; [[thm-tychonoff]] inherits
   107	that use and spends it a second time directly, to produce a point of a product of
   108	nonempty sets; and [[fs-compact-implies-sequentially-compact]] inherits both,
   109	since its witness is compact by Tychonoff. Tychonoff's theorem **implies** the
   110	Axiom of Choice, so, under the standing assumption that ZF is consistent, no proof
   111	of it in ZF alone can exist; the exact form of
   112	that implication, and the correction of the classical derivation, are recorded in
   113	[[rem-schechter-kelley-tychonoff]], which this library states and does not prove.
   114	Where the ultrafilter lemma sits between the two is [[rem-choice-strengths]].
   115	
   116	**Spending the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]).** Claims 2
   117	and 4 of [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]], each of which picks a point
   118	outside each of countably many nested unions; claim 3 of
   119	[[thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness]]; claims 2 and 4 of
   120	[[thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact]]; and the two false
   121	statements whose witnesses those are,
   122	[[fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact]] and
   123	[[fs-countably-compact-implies-compact]]. In the ordinal and long-ray results the
   124	principle enters through a boundedness theorem for at most countable
   125	subsets, which carries the hypothesis in its own statement — and not only
   126	through it: claim 2 of
   127	[[thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact]] spends it once more
   128	directly, to pick a point in each of countably many nonempty sets, and claim 3
   129	of [[thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness]] inherits a further use through claim 2
   130	of [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]].
   131	
   132	**Spending the Axiom of Dependent Choice ([[def-dependent-choice]]).** Claim 3 of
   133	[[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]], where it is spent to extract a countably
   134	infinite subset from an infinite set, which is not a theorem of ZF; the claim
   135	about countably infinite subsets alone, claim 1(d) of the same theorem, is free
   136	of that cost. And [[thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff]], which spends
   137	it once, through [[lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations]], to run a
   138	shrinking construction whose admissible successors change with the stage. In both
   139	cases dependent choice is an upper bound on the cost of the argument given here
   140	and is not asserted to be necessary; for the Baire theorem in particular the
   141	several versions of the statement correspond to different principles over ZF, as
   142	[[rem-baire-category-choice-strength]] records.
   143	
   144	**The metric ledger is separate and remains in force.** What each implication
   145	between the compactness properties of a *metric* space costs is recorded in
   146	[[rem-compactness-choice-ledger-metric]]. Nothing here supersedes it: by
   147	[[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]] the metric statements are the
   148	statements of this page read in a metric topology, so the two ledgers describe the
   149	same arrows wherever they overlap and different arrows elsewhere.
   150	
   151	**A warning about equivalences.** [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]] is
   152	deliberately stated as a list of implications rather than as one equivalence,
   153	because an equivalence proved by going round a cycle charges every arrow in it the
   154	maximum cost. The same discipline is what
   155	[[rem-compactness-choice-ledger-metric]] exists to enforce on the metric side.
